  :global(html[data-motion='failed']) .hero-crt {
    animation: none;
  }
  :global(html[data-motion='reduced']) .hero-crt::before,
  :global(html[data-motion='reduced']) .hero-crt::after,
  :global(html[data-motion='failed']) .hero-crt::before,
  :global(html[data-motion='failed']) .hero-crt::after {
    display: none;
  }

  /*
    The hero's signal glitch on Technical / Roots — the analog-TV look: the
    word steady almost all the time, then a short burst where it loses sync.
    Three layers do it, matching the reference set (whole-word chromatic split,
    torn bright slices, pixel debris):

    - `.outage::before/::after` — whole copies of the word in the accent blue
      and purple, *behind* it, thrown a few px apart. Mostly the full word
      (that is the chromatic aberration), sometimes only a band of it.
    - `.outage__tear::before/::after` — slices of the word in ink, *in front*,
      thrown 6–18px sideways. One is solid, one is a 3px scanline raster (the
      CRT pitch from BreakoutCTA), which is the textured band in the refs.
    - `.debris` — short bars scattered round the words, two groups per word
      strobing on alternate frames.

    The keyframes were generated once from a seeded LCG (so the values are
    arbitrary but fixed, like the Breakout rack) and are plain CSS from here
    on: edit frames directly. Every frame inside a burst is ~25ms. Every
    layer of a word shares its duration and delay, so all three land in the same
    burst windows and are fully off between them. The two words run on clocks
    that share no factor (7.3s / 11.9s), so they never fall into a loop.

    `steps(1, end)` throughout: a lost signal cuts, it does not ease. The main
    word dims its *colour*, not its opacity — every layer is its descendant,
    and an opacity dip would take the glitch down with it.

    SplitText puts its word wrapper inside `.outage__tear`; the reveal tween
    only moves that wrapper, so it never touches these properties. The `/ ""`
    on `content` gives each copy empty alt text, or a screen reader would read
    the word five times. Reduced motion and failed motion remove it entirely —
    a strobing headline is exactly what that setting exists to stop.
  */
  .outage,
  .outage__tear {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-block;
  }
  .outage {
    animation: var(--glitch-main) var(--glitch-dur) steps(1, end) var(--glitch-delay) infinite;
  }
  .outage--a {
    --glitch-dur: 7.3s;
    --glitch-delay: 2.4s;
    --glitch-main: outage-a;
  }
  .outage--b {
    --glitch-dur: 11.9s;
    --glitch-delay: 3.7s;
    --glitch-main: outage-b;
  }

  .outage::before,
  .outage::after,
  .outage__tear::before,
  .outage__tear::after {
    content: attr(data-text);
    content: attr(data-text) / '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: 0;
    text-shadow: none;
  }

  /* Chromatic copies, behind the word. */
  .outage::before,
  .outage::after {
    z-index: -1;
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }
  .outage::before {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    -webkit-text-fill-color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .outage::after {
    color: var(--color-accent-warm);
    -webkit-text-fill-color: var(--color-accent-warm);
  }
  .outage--a::before { animation: rgb-a1 var(--glitch-dur) steps(1, end) var(--glitch-delay) infinite; }
  .outage--a::after { animation: rgb-a2 var(--glitch-dur) steps(1, end) var(--glitch-delay) infinite; }
  .outage--b::before { animation: rgb-b1 var(--glitch-dur) steps(1, end) var(--glitch-delay) infinite; }
  .outage--b::after { animation: rgb-b2 var(--glitch-dur) steps(1, end) var(--glitch-delay) infinite; }

  /* Torn slices, in front. */
  .outage__tear::before {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    -webkit-text-fill-color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .outage__tear::after {
    color: transparent;
    -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    background-image: repeating-linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      var(--color-ink) 0 2px,
      transparent 2px 3px
    );
    background-clip: text;
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
  }
  .outage--a .outage__tear::before { animation: tear-a1 var(--glitch-dur) steps(1, end) var(--glitch-delay) infinite; }
  .outage--a .outage__tear::after { animation: tear-a2 var(--glitch-dur) steps(1, end) var(--glitch-delay) infinite; }
  .outage--b .outage__tear::before { animation: tear-b1 var(--glitch-dur) steps(1, end) var(--glitch-delay) infinite; }
  .outage--b .outage__tear::after { animation: tear-b2 var(--glitch-dur) steps(1, end) var(--glitch-delay) infinite; }

